Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Beliefs are rules of action, in other words, they dictate a specific set of actions that must be taken in accordance with the belief.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This defines what it means to have a belief, and allows for the construction of the pragmatic maxim.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>A belief that “it is raining,” for instance, leads us to some change in our behavior, such as prompting us to shelter in place or bring an umbrella. However, there is no fundamental difference between the claim that “it is raining,” and “it is raining acid, except the acid is impossible to be detected in anyway and for all intents and purposes, is identical to rain.” Since the latter would not necessitate a difference in conduct, there is no difference between the two claims.</p><h4>Chinese Philosophy</h4><p>The schools of philosophy that originated in China likewise also display a view like this towards epistemology. In fact, they (with the exception of Buddhism, introduced from India), had no formal study of epistemology, so a pragmatic stance towards knowledge was assumed.</p><h4>References</h4>","A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes majoritarianism over utilitarianism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A system that elects a Condorcet Winner prioritizes ensuring the will of the majority, and not ensuring the that outcome is optimal in the utilitarian sense.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Because electing a Condorcet Winner is only concerned with electing the leader who beats all other candidates individually, it does not take into consideration the level of support that the candidate has relative to other candidates.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Where as Condorcet Winner are merely the candidates who are supported by the most people, a winner elected via Score Voting ensures that the winner selected maximizes social welfare.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">Score Voting ensures that the outcome of an election maximizes social utility</span><ul><li>The alternative voting method that contrasts with a system that seeks a Condorcet Winner.</li></ul></li></ul>","All the King's Men":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows Jack Burden, a cynical journalist who becomes the aide and confidant of Willie Stark, an ambitious politician who rises from a small-town reformer to the powerful governor of a Southern state. As Stark gains influence, he becomes increasingly corrupt, using manipulation, intimidation, and political deals to achieve his goals, while Jack investigates the secrets and scandals of those around him. Jack’s search into the past of Judge Irwin, a respected public official, reveals hidden connections and forces him to confront his own family history and moral responsibility. After Stark’s actions lead to tragedy and betrayal, both he and Jack face the consequences of their choices. The novel explores power, corruption, guilt, ambition, and the idea that everyone is connected to the past.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>A book the explores politics in an extremely in-depth way and which provides a close look into the philosophical underpinnings of populism.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Power corrupts those who possess it\">Power corrupts those who possess it</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos\">People are far more easily swayed by pathos than logos</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People sin so that they may be loved\">People sin so that they may be loved</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"People are a product of their times and circumstance\">People are a product of their times and circumstance</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Society corrupted innately good humans\">Society corrupted innately good humans</a></li></ul><h4>Questions</h4><ul><li>Is Adam Stanton a good person? The book presents us with a dilemma: is someone’s moral stance improved more by the nature of their actions, or the final result? Do the ends justify the means?</li></ul><h4>Characters</h4><p><strong>Willie Stark</strong></p><ul><li>One seems to be able to divide his life into multiple stages:<ol><li><em>Ex ante</em>: he was innocent and his worldview was limited. In a stroke of luck, through family connections, he becomes the town treasurer. He, however, loses the school house fight and his political career sinks.</li><li>Enlightenment: after he is vindicated on the school house issue and receiving Tiny Duffy’s offer, Stark gained ambition. He was genuinely invested with a divine calling and tried to spread his message to the voters, though the masses did not care. They were unable to see the light which shone so brightly in Stark’s mind. It is ultimately due to Burden and Burke that Stark regains his footing.</li><li>Post-enlightenment: his fantastical worldview is shattered after he learns that the reason for his divine calling was merely to split the vote of another political candidate. He gains a sense of stubbornness and a will that turns him into a Machiavellian character who is willing to do anything in order to, somewhat ironically, serve the public good.<ul><li>This is a worldview that he advocates to his disheartened and apathetic consituency.</li></ul></li></ol></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 133</p><p>This is the truth: you are a hick and nobody ever helped a hick but the hick himself. Up there in town they won’t help you. <strong>It is up to you and God, and God helps those who help themselves.</strong></p></blockquote><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 175</p><p>“He's interested in Willie. Quite simply and directly. <strong>And when anybody is interested in himself quite simply and directly the way Willie is interested in Willie you call it genius.</strong> It’s only the half-baked people like Mr. Patton who are interested in money Even the big boys who make a real lot of money aren’t interested in money. Henry Ford isn't interested in money. He's interested in Henry Ford and therefore he is a genius.”</p></blockquote><p><strong>Jack Burden</strong></p><ul><li>Is able to see the situation in a more objective light than Stark. He begins to see that Stark’s quest is fundamentally rudderless and empty in the middle.<ul><li>In his “first excursion,” he looked naively for some sense in the world, some justice, but he found none. The facts, he realized, are much more important than the (moral) truth.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 217</p><p>It was to be my second excursion into the past, more interesting and sensational than the first, and much more successful. In fact, this second excursion into the past was to be perfectly successful. But the first one had not been successful. It had not been successful because <strong>in the midst of the process I tried to discover the truth and not the facts.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>It seems like at the end of college, Burden went through a period of his life characterized by the same listlessness that characterized period’s of Stark’s life, and he eventually found a purpose (his own divine calling) in working for Stark.<ul><li>It seems like maybe he is fed up with “thinking”? He often makes rhetorical, equivocational arguments which seem merely to look like arguments. Often, he does not even think out loud. Burden seems to be the embodiment of a disillusioned academic.</li><li>He finds Stark’s searching for the truth in his “heart” to be a welcome change to his laborious academic methods.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 139</p><p>For God and Nothing have a lot in common. <strong>You look either one of Them straight in the eye for a second and the immediate effect on the human constitution is the same.</strong></p></blockquote><p><strong>Adam Stanton</strong></p><ul><li>Wants to be someone who is morally in the right every time. He does not care about the outcome of something as much as that the process is done in a way that is morally acceptable.<ul><li>This is the reason he has so much reluctance when Stark asked him to head his hospital.</li><li>But I think the fact that Adam eventually joined the hospital I think in a way undermines his character: he joins the hospital once he finds out about his father taking a bribe, since at that point, there is no more purpose to maintaining his moral purity in the same way.</li><li>But if this is the case, I feel like his original moral principles were not worth holding on to in the first place. It seems like he did not believe them <em>for themselves</em>, but rather, as a heuristic for outward action. Why should it matter to him that his father took a bribe? It’s something that’s already been done, so there is no reason for him to let that disturb his moral principles.</li></ul></li><li>Adam values keeping himself in a morally pure world. This is the circumstance under which he refuses to do things for a utilitarian benefit.</li><li>Jack Burden explicates Adam’s flaws most succintly here:</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, p. 598</p><p>I tried to tell her how if you could not accept the past and its burden there was no future, for without one there cannot be the other, and how if you could accept the past you might hope for the future, for only out of the past can you make the future.</p></blockquote><h4>Note</h4><ul><li>“Love” seems to be entirely absent from the book. There is no relationship between any of the characters which can, genuinely, be categorized as a relationship built on love. All relationships are <em>quid pro quo</em> or merely out of habit.<ul><li>This changes later in the book, with the two true “loves” between Jack and Anne and Jack’s mother and Judge Irwin it seems.</li><li>In this, even, I think the book is making a commentary on the nature of love. Jack often states the importance of truth, and how truth underlies everything, but it seems that love is the one that that can muddle truth.</li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, page 50</p><p>All he wants is to have that child sit in a chair for a couple of hours and then go off to bed under the same roof. It’s not love. I am not saying that there is not such a thing as love. I am merely pointing to something which is different from love but which sometimes goes by the name of love. It may well be that without this thing which I am talking about there would not be any love. <strong>But this thing in itself is not love. It is just something in the blood. It is a kind of blood greed, and it is the fate of a man.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>Willie Stark and Adam Stanton represents the two opposite ways of dealing with the existence of evil in the world. Stark channels it in order to do good, and Stanton shys away from it.<ul><li>When the two inevitably butt heads, the result is destruction.</li><li>And it seems that both methods have their good sides and bad sides. <ul><li>Willie: although he is able to do good in his state, to do so, he often had to resort to very coercive or ignoble actions. By doing things in this reckless manner, Stark lost his son, and eventually his own life.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">Message of the Book?</p><p>It seems that what the book is trying to say is that both types of men are needed in the world. With only Willie Starks, the world would combust under the multitudinous, bull-headed pressure, exerted from all sides and with no regards to collateral damage. With only Adam Stantons, nothing impressive will ever get done.</p></blockquote><ul><li>All the characters in the book are single minded in what they want. Once they find a goal, they stop questioning whether its the right thing the do, instead, solely focusing on achieving the desired outcome.</li><li>At its core, this book seems to be some form of a critique of post-modernism, showing the chaos and psychological instability that it engenders.</li></ul><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All The King’s Men</em>, p. 541</p><p>More money for graft, the opposition always screamed. \"Sure,' the Boss had said, lounging easy, “<strong>sure, there's some graft, but there's just enough to make the wheels turn without squeaking. And remember this. There never was a machine rigged up by man didn't represent some loss of energy.</strong>”</p></blockquote><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\"><em>All the King’s Men</em>, p. 485</p><p>For either killing or creating may be a crime punishable by death, and the death always comes by the criminal's own hand and every man is a suicide.</p></blockquote><ul><li>This is how everybody in the book died: Stark, Irwin, Adam Stanton, Mortimer, etc.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a><ul><li>Touches on similar themes regarding fate and epistemology.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hamlet (play)\">Hamlet (play)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"East of Eden\">East of Eden</a><ul><li>This book almost seems like the opposite of East of Eden, not in terms of messages (for it seems that both books are fundamentally getting at the same thing), but one is told with the Yang and the other the Ying and the their message is the one and the same, and that constitutes the Ying and the Yang.</li></ul></li></ul>","Classical compatibilism locates unfreedom in external impediment rather than in determinism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>On the classical compatibilist analysis, what opposes freedom is not causal determination but obstruction, so an agent is free whenever nothing external prevents them from doing what they want to do.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the analysis that makes freedom cheap enough to survive determinism, and every later compatibilism is a repair to it.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Hobbes gives the canonical formulation, defining liberty as the absence of impediments to action not contained in the nature of the agent. The analysis has an immediate cost: it secures the freedom to act otherwise if one wanted to, but says nothing about the freedom to want otherwise, and the attempt to add that generates a regress, since changing one’s will would require a prior will to change it.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will\">The conditional analysis of ability defines free action rather than free will</a><ul><li>The precise form of the failure described above.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom\">One cannot voluntarily choose to lose one’s freedom</a><ul><li>A constraint any account of freedom has to accommodate.</li></ul></li></ul>","Confucianism has a prominent focus on education":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An important facet of Confucianism is its focus on the importance of education, reflected in the Civil Service exams (see The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy).</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Confucianism’s focus on education allowed for Chinese state institutions to be operated effectively, and for its civilization to produce a variety of advancements.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions\">The scholar-gentry embodied Confucianism in imperial Chinese state institutions</a><ul><li>The human product of that focus: officials who were also scholars.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state\">The Chinese imperial examination system allowed for political integration of elites into the state</a><ul><li>Complicates this note — the exams served co-optation as much as education.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy\">The Imperial Chinese Civil Service Exams represent a meritocracy</a></li></ul>","Developing countries can be stuck in the middle income trap":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Developing nations, when they emerge out of low-income status, may get trapped in the so-called “middle income trap,” unable to join the ranks of the high-income nations.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a common issue for developing nations, as they can remain stuck in the middle income trap if they do not successfully escape the trap.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Nations can be stuck in the middle income trap for many reasons:</p><ol><li>Lack of required technologies needed to advance to high-income status.</li><li>Lack of strong rule of law, means that long-term growth is difficult.</li><li>Lack of human capital.</li><li>Reliance on commodities, which means the country relies on selling raw materials rather than developing a diversified economic base.</li></ol><h4>Successes</h4><p>Countries like the Four Asian Tigers were able to escape the trap due to their investments in education and high tech industries.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><span class=\"wl-off\">The 'Resource Curse' can often prevent nations from attaining high-income status</span><ul><li>One of the reasons listed in this note for a reason that a country may be trapped in middle-income status.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Examples of economics policies adopted by the nations that successfully escaped the middle income trap.</li></ul></li></ul>","Free will and responsibility are not mutually exclusive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It is often claimed that it would be really difficult to establish claims about responsibility in the case where people don’t have free will, though this note argues otherwise.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>If this note is the case, then we could potentially make the determinist worldview more likely in the face of its unlikeliness due to the exact problem that this note highlights before.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There have been many arguments made to prove this point:</p><ol><li>Peter Strawson puts forward the thesis of “reactive attitudes,” which means that responsibility is found in the appropriate change in treatment or attitude towards an offender based on their breach of a relationship.</li></ol><h4>References</h4>","Given that basic rights are protected, inequality ought to be reduced as much as possible":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Rawls’ famous idea that is related to his Veil of Ignorance. Essentially places basic rights and freedoms as the first-order requirement that must be satisfied before attempting to reduce inequality.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the fundamental axiom that underlies Rawls’ political and moral philosophy.</p><h4>References</h4>","High levels of tribalism is associated with lower levels of democracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Higher levels of tribalism and patronage is associated with lower levels of democracy, since society like this are typically less able to cooperate on a larger scale.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains why countries which are much more tribalistic have trouble adopting democracy.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Specific proxies, such as cousin marriage, can be pointed to show the effects of tribalism on democracy. In fact, there is a high negative correlation between cousin marriage and the level of democracy. Typically, collectivist societies are the ones with higher levels of tribalism, and they perform worse on many of the metrics by which we judge a country.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Collectivist countries are more lonely less individualist ones\">Collectivist countries are more lonely less individualist ones</a><ul><li>Another point on collectivist countries.</li></ul></li></ul>","Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>East Asian countries have higher levels of intelligence on average, which explains the success of these countries. It is not their industrial policy, as is commonly claimed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This refutes the common idea that it is industrial policy that allowed East Asian countries to prosper.</p><h4>GDP per capita PPP vs. PISA</h4><p></p><h4>References</h4>","Inequality has dropped globally, though increased within countries":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Inequality has not skyrocketed in the world, despite what is often claimed in the media.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This disproves a common myth that is spread that inequality has been continually rising around the globe, but supports the idea that inequality has increased intra-nation.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p> Since the early 2000s, and especially with the 2008 Financial Crisis, inequality has been falling across the world. Global income inequality has also been falling. At the same time, inequality has risen in individual countries. </p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement\">Inequality is caused by technological innovation overtaking human capital improvement</a><ul><li>A potential cause of the inequality is outlined.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health\">Economic inequality does not necessarily harm psychological health</a><ul><li>A fallacious claim about inequality is that it harms mental health.</li></ul></li></ul>","Justice is the full set of virtues that relate to how one engages with others under the law":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Justice is considered by Aristotle to be the “sovereign virtue,” since it encompasses all of the virtues that he has described, and it regards the way that one interacts with others under the law.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is Aristotle’s definition of universal/general justice.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Justice the process of aiming at the mean state, while injustice is aiming at the extremes. There is a difference between the concept of justice and “political virtue,” despite both terms regard the stance of the individual towards the regime. Full political justice, justice as the common advantage, is achieved only in a correct regime, and perfectly only in the best regime where civic virtue and complete human virtue coincide. But personal justice does not require a good regime, and the mere presence of civic virtue guarantees nothing, because it is exactly the kind of virtue a bad regime can put to work.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Law is not constitutive of justice\">Law is not constitutive of justice</a><ul><li>A refutation of this idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","Oedipus at Colonus":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Follows the blind, exiled Oedipus as he arrives at Colonus with Antigone and seeks sanctuary at a sacred site, where he confronts his suffering, fate, and past crimes. As Creon tries to persuade—or pressure—him to return to Thebes, Oedipus refuses, distrusting his motives, and the conflict escalates into threats against his daughters. Theseus, king of Athens, offers protection, and the play explores themes of justice, mercy, redemption, and the moral duty to protect the vulnerable.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This play furthers exploration of the themes that were present in Oedipus Rex and explores from new themes as well.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Humanity is insignificant\">Humanity is insignificant</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy\">Tragedy can be more optimistic than comedy</a></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Oedipus the King (play)\">Oedipus the King (play)</a></li></ul>","Pleasure perfects activities, and thus, life":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Pleasure accompanies and completes and activity when a well-functioning faculty, perceptual or intellectual, is active towards its appropriate object.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This describes the value/meaning of pleasure.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Pleasure perfects an activity, making the activity better and more fully realized than it would otherwise be.  Thus, pleasure perfects life for the individual, since it perfects activities, and life is an activity. <em>When we feel pleasure:</em></p><ul><li>We don’t constantly feel pleasure due to fatigue. </li><li>Or, something, we only felt pleasure at something for its novelty, which, after it wears off, means that we no longer feel the same pleasure.</li></ul><p><em>How pleasure can change:</em></p><ul><li>Different activities produce different pleasures, since they involve different actions.<ul><li>Pleasure intensifies their respective activities, increasing enjoyment and allowing us to improve at it.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure is not solely the Good\">Pleasure is not solely the Good</a><ul><li>This being said, pleasure is not the sole aim/good of life.</li></ul></li></ul>","Political attitudes is one's opinion on what political change should occur, how fast, and how much":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Political attitude is one’s opinions on the degree of political change, how much of it should occur, and how fast it should occur. It can determine the direction towards which a society advances.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant for understanding under political system, since understanding political attitude allows for the understanding of why a political shift is occurring when it is occurring.</p><h4>References</h4>","Population decline makes markets less competitive":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Population decline has made markets less competitive and more prone to concentration of power by a few.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Another reason why population decline would be bad for economies beyond the macro effect (such as the effect on the labor market).</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>The correlation between labor force growth and startup entry rate is 0.74.</li><li>There are less entry into markets, the average firm size has been increasing, and the share of young firms is decreasing.</li><li>Without new firms/ideas, there is less creative destructions, and so firms hold onto products longer and allow markups to drift up.</li><li>The economy also in the process loses from the new variety creation that comes with new entrants into markets.</li><li>This calls for greater anti trust laws in order to prevent the formation of monopolies.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rising populations in the mid-1700s led to social discontent\">Rising populations in the mid-1700s led to social discontent</a><ul><li>At the same time, perhaps it had the opposite effect as described in this note?</li></ul></li></ul>","Psychological continuity exists on a continuum":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The idea that the continuity of existence is something that exists on a spectrum. As we collect new experience and loss old experience, we thus gradually become more distant from our original self.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that as people's acquired stimulus changes, we slowly become different people, and this solves the problem of \"when we become a different\" person after some period of time.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>Parfit argues that:<ul><li>Personal identity isn‘t what we should care about from a self-interested perspective.</li><li>We should care about there existing a future being psychologically connected with us in the right way, in that the future self have some of the same values, personality, beliefs, and intentions.<ul><li>What matters comes in degrees: whether someone is psychologically connected to you in this way is a matter of degree.<ul><li>This psychological distance is the result of the build up of new experiences (or loss) not time.</li></ul></li><li>You at 15 and 70 are more different than between you at 15 and 30.<ul><li>We can‘t have the conscience to look forwards to the future, because you will not be the same person.</li></ul></li><li>An implication of this view is that you have a moral responsibility for your future self.</li></ul></li><li>A good analogy for this can be found in Sorites Paradox.<ul><li>It is a gradation of you. Its like instead of define a “pile” you only define “x degree of a pile”. This is the reduction. There is no true “pile”.</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","Recognizing one’s failures late does not by itself constitute redemption":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Clear sight of what one has done wrong is a cognitive achievement rather than a moral one, and treating the recognition as though it discharged the failure understates what the failure cost.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This qualifies the common literary claim that dying clarifies a life, since clarity about a wasted life is compatible with the life having been wasted.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The ending of <em>The Snows of Kilimanjaro</em> can be read either way. The flight to the summit is unitalicised, which invites reading it as genuine deliverance; but Hemingway never revises Harry’s judgment of himself, and a redemption granted for the recognition alone would be too cheap for the life that preceded it. The ironic reading is the stronger one, and the text sustains both.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Only when moribund do we have a clear perspective on our own life\">Only when moribund do we have a clear perspective on our own life</a><ul><li>The claim this qualifies.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Small neglected problems compound into the shape of a whole life\">Small neglected problems compound into the shape of a whole life</a><ul><li>Why the recognition arrives too late to do any work.</li></ul></li></ul>","States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>For states that surround a rising hegemon, they can choose between two options:</p><ul><li>Bandwagon, or</li><li>Balance.</li></ul><h4>Significance</h4><p>This provides a framework for thinking about how states surrounding rising powers can respond to the rising power.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>If states bandwagon, it means that they join with the rising power. This demands that surrounding states have trust in the rising power that it does not simply consume the surrounding states.</p><ul><li>This is more likely within civilizations, though the likelihood of this happening still depends on the specific civilization.</li></ul><p>If states balance, they will do so through the formation of alliances that oppose the rising power (primary balancers). Huntington argues that with the rise of China, states are likely to be the former and not the latter, while the norm has been the latter in European history.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a><ul><li>The reason that things will most likely be the former is due to China's historical cultural and economic and political influence on surrounding states.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Dutch Angle can be used to convey a sense of disorientation and confusion with the surrounding world":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Dutch Angle can be used in files in order to show the disorientation of a viewpoint, or character, or world.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a way to elicit feelings of discomfort in the viewer through the use of cinematographical techniques.</p><h4>Examples</h4><p>The Dutch Angle is used extensively in <em>The Third Man</em>.  </p><h4>References</h4>","The Old South was reluctant to change after the Civil War":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The Antebellum South was reluctant to change and accept a new position in the world after their defeat in the Civil War, which manifested in ways both culturally and politically.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the persistence of many Antebellum features of the South well past the end of the Civil War.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This was explored through literary treatment of the topic, for instance through <em>TSTF</em>:</p><ul><li>One reading of the book is that it is 3 failed attempts to tell one story is metaphorical of the fact that the three brothers represent three ways the South should not deal with its trauma.<ul><li>Benji is stuck in the past, with no conception of the future. He is literally castrated.</li><li>Quentin is stuck mourning the past in the present, and this mourning drives him to commit suicide.</li><li>Jason is able to see the future, but his worldview is incredibly cynical and he sees the world as being cosmically unjust. He is unable to come to terms with past, present, or future.</li></ul></li><li>All three characters struggle with a loss of innocence/purity.<ul><li>Benji is unable to process this, and thus is the least affected.</li><li>Quentin kills himself because he realizes such a loss is permanent and there is nothing he can do.</li><li>Jason fights against the world and tries his best to preserve his purity/honor, but fails.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Polybius’ Histories is a 40-book work that explains how Rome rose from a regional power to dominate the Mediterranean in little more than a century, especially through the aftermath of the Second Punic War and the defeat of Macedon and Carthage. Polybius argues that this success was not accidental but came from Rome’s mixed constitution, military discipline, political institutions, and ability to absorb and adapt to change. He writes as a Greek statesman and hostage living in Rome, so the work also serves as a practical guide to understanding power, causation, and the interconnected nature of world events, with a strong emphasis on eyewitness inquiry, political realism, and lessons for future leaders.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the main sources for historians with regards to the rise of the Roman Republic.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><h5>Historical Narrative</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Hannibal was an excellent tactician, but poor grand strategist\">Hannibal was an excellent tactician, but poor grand strategist</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Divided leadership compromises the effectiveness of an army\">Divided leadership compromises the effectiveness of an army</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Cavalry were effective in ancient warfare because of the mobility asymmetry that they created\">Cavalry were effective in ancient warfare because of the mobility asymmetry that they created</a></li></ul><h5>Political &amp; Military Insights</h5><p>Polybius argues that political systems follow this cycle:</p><ol><li>People follow the strongest leader, and one leader eventually starts a <strong>kingship</strong> where they are virtuous. </li><li>The kingship deteriorates into a <strong>tyranny</strong> and the people overthrow it.</li><li>The people establish an <strong>aristocracy</strong>, which is likewise corrupted and becomes an <strong>oligarchy</strong>.</li><li>People overthrow the oligarchy and establish a <strong>democracy</strong>.</li><li>The democracy eventually deteriorates into anarchy, and the cycle repeats.</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction\">The Roman Republican political system is incredibly stable due to its composite construction</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Romans learned from the people they conquered\">The Romans learned from the people they conquered</a></li></ol><h4>Tactics Compendium</h4><ul><li>The use both a contravallation and circumvallation in the Siege of Agrigentum by the Romans, a tactic that would later by employed by Caesar during the Gallic Wars (specifically the Siege of Alesia).</li><li>The use of the <em>corvus</em>, a long, hinged stick with a metal hook at the end, which would have been used against Carthaginian vessels to pull them closer to the Roman vessels and thus neutralize the power of their fleet.</li><li>Hannibal’s crossing of the Alps and the Arno Marshes, which allowed him to penetrate deep into Roman territory without encountering much resistance.</li><li>At the Battle of the Trebia, Hannibal ensures that when the time came for the armies to engage, while the Romans had to ford a river and were left without eating breakfast, the Carthaginians were well-prepared.</li><li>At the Battle of Cannae, the use of a convex line which drew the Romans in and eventually produced a concave shape that allowed for the envelopment of the Roman army.</li><li>At the Battle of Ager Falernus, although Hannibal was trapped in the Capuan valleys, he was able to use a herd of oxen with torches attached to their horns in order to distract the Romans and escape.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","The congruence problem is easier solved by contract theories rather than utilitarian ones":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The congruence problem is easier solved with contract theories than utilitarian ones since the utilitarian view is too demanding.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is part of the argument for a contract view and against utilitarianism.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>There are two ways in which utilitarianism is too demanding:</p><ol><li>It requires too much sacrifice in order to maximize aggregate gains. It means that one could have to sacrifice, theoretically, nearly all of their ability to accomplish their plan of life in order to maximize the overall utility.</li><li>Utilitarianism makes no guarantee of the basic level of rights that an individual should have, meaning that if it maximizes utility to remove some people of their rights, it would be justifiable to do so. It seems that a rational person would hardly agree to something like this.<ul><li>Having rights and abiding by the principles of justice is fundamentally regulative under contract theories.</li></ul></li></ol><p>Additionally, utilitarianism cannot account for things like love, which may cause individuals to act in non-utility-maximizing ways.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism\">The difference principle has definite advantages over utilitarianism</a><ul><li>Another branch of Rawls’ attack against utilitarianism.</li></ul></li></ul>","The goal of thought is to generate belief":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The goal of thinking is in order to form beliefs, which are regulations for action.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the very fundamentals of thinking and what it means to think.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Since the goal of thinking is in order to generate belief, and belief is a specific course of action that we should take in specific circumstances, it means that differences in meaning which motivate no differences in action are not different beliefs, and thus we should not waste our time in thinking about them.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act\">A distinction in meaning is only extant given some actual change in the way we act</a><ul><li>Draws on this definition of beliefs.</li></ul></li></ul>","The pursuit of complete certainty delays action past the point where it can succeed":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>An agent who treats certainty as a precondition for acting will keep gathering evidence past the point at which the evidence was already sufficient, and the delay itself produces the outcome the agent was trying to avoid.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This gives a failure mode of epistemic conscientiousness, which is otherwise a virtue, and it explains why the most careful agent in a situation is often the one who is destroyed by it.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>Hamlet is driven throughout the play by a relentless pursuit of the truth about his father’s death. The clues accumulate to the point of being overwhelming, yet he does not act on them, choosing instead to gather more, and it is that delay rather than any error of judgment that produces the catastrophe.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>A different cost of the same pursuit.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a><ul><li>The faculty that is being over-exercised here.</li></ul></li></ul>","There is fierce domestic competition between Chinese AI firms":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While in the US there is often talk about how to face the Chinese AI strategy, in fact, no such strategy exists. There is extremely fierce competition between AI firms in China; there is no large scale cooperation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This addresses a common misconception regarding the nature of Chinese AI.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>What is experience in the US is only the “exhaust” of the internal AI fight between the domestic Chinese companies, so it is a misconception to talk about Chinese AI as being unified in any way.</p><ul><li>The high level of competition, explains the ultra-low token pricing and the distillation of American models.</li><li>There is also extensive government control over firms in China, such that the government can simply prevent M&amp;A deals to prevent Chinese companies from going to the West (e.g., Manus).</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"More Chinese AI firms might be founded in the US\">More Chinese AI firms might be founded in the US</a><ul><li>This is a consequence of the fact that there is extensive government control over firms in China, so more firms might be founded in the US, where it is less arbitrary.</li></ul></li></ul>","What is pleasurable to the good man is truly pleasurable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle allows that pleasures vary with people’s character, but he holds that the virtuous person is the measure of what is pleasant without qualification, so the pleasures that accompany virtuous activities are genuinely pleasant and therefore choice worthy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains that not all pleasures are good/desirable.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>There are certain pleasures, such as bodily ones, which may appeal to certain individuals.</li><li>However, what is truly worthy striving for are the things that bring about pleasure based on the character of the virtuous person.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Acting virtuously is not painful\">Acting virtuously is not painful</a> <ul><li>In fact, the virtuous man enjoys many pleasures, and these pleasures that they enjoy are the ones that are genuinely pleasant.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state\">For an act to be virtuous, the action must have a quality of virtuousness, and the agent must also act in a virtuous state</a><ul><li>In acting in a virtuous state, the agent gains pleasure, which is the highest pleasure possible.</li></ul></li></ul>"});
